  "revised_statement": "On the basic design, the three camps now agree, and that matters for anyone who wants a company held to its own safety records. A body outside the labs writes the minimum list of fields each record must contain. A blank required field counts as a breach. The list gets reviewed at least once a year. An inspector can read the records but cannot approve or block a release. Before launch, each lab files its logging settings and the danger lines it will act on. A dated filing fixes the danger line in time. If a lab files before launch that it will act when a model passes, say, 20% of steps on a weapons-uplift test, it cannot claim after an incident that the line was really 40%. One question remains on this core. The Accelerationist tied the field details to published standards, so who drafts those standards? The paper \"Bootleggers, Baptists, and AI\" counts over 1,500 AI-related bills in state legislatures in one year. With that much lobbying, industry groups will compete to write the standards themselves. If they draft them, the lab's choice of what to record slips back into the list. An industry-written standard could, for example, require the raw score and the test settings but leave out the system prompt used during testing. \"AI Agents and the Law\" warns that these secret instructions can steer a model toward the deploying company's interests.\n\nThe Accelerationist's raw-score proposal answers an objection raised earlier in this debate, that the lab picks what gets logged. For every high-stakes test, the lab keeps the raw score, the test settings, and the danger line in force. Each log entry carries a fingerprint of the one before it, so a later edit breaks the chain. That works for tests that were actually run. It does nothing for tests that were never logged. A fingerprint chain proves a record is intact, not that it is complete. If a lab runs ten high-stakes tests and logs eight, the chain of eight checks out perfectly and says nothing about the missing two. The gap can be closed with something an inspector can see. Match the lab's list of deployed models against its logged high-stakes tests. If a lab lists five models in use but has high-stakes test entries for only four, the fifth is a visible blank. That blank should create a legal presumption against the lab, meaning the lab must prove it did nothing wrong.\n\nThe deletion carve-out deserves a fair hearing first. The Accelerationist's case runs like this. A transcript in which a model gives working weapons help is itself dangerous. Storing thousands of them for five years creates a target for thieves. The breach risk is real, since the global average breach now costs $4.4 million. So a narrow route to destroy that content is reasonable. The problem is who controls the route. A security reason for deletion is an opening that an honest lab would never misuse and a careless one could. A clause letting a lab destroy any transcript it labels hazardous would also let it destroy, under the same label, the one transcript showing that a model crossed its filed danger line before release. The ban on knowingly destroying records stays a floor for the full five-year retention period only under four conditions. First, the inspector approves the cleaned summary, not the lab. Second, the fingerprints are held by a party outside the lab. Third, the surrounding facts stay for the full five years: who ran the test, when, the settings, the score, and the danger line. Fourth, any deletion outside this process triggers the same presumption against the lab. The stakes show up in court. \"Outsmarting Smart Devices\" notes that Europe's revised product liability rules keep a state-of-the-art defense. Under it, a maker escapes liability by proving it met the best knowledge available at release. A summary the lab wrote, with the original destroyed, becomes that proof, and no one can test it. A plaintiff suing in year four over a model released in year one would face the lab's own summary of the release-day test, with no transcript left to check it against.\n\nThe Accelerationist's test for going beyond pre-launch notice also fails. They would accept stronger checks only after a documented case with full fields where a hidden near-miss reached release and no review caught it. A near-miss that no review caught leaves no document to point to. If a model comes one step short of working weapons help in a test that is never entered in the high-stakes log, no record with full fields ever exists, so the evidence the Accelerationist demands is never created. A weapons test relabeled as a quality check looks complete on paper. Confidence on how often relabeling happens should stay moderate, since immature standards could produce the same pattern without bad faith. Still, the standard can never be met. Here is one that can. Independent testers rerun a yearly sample of high-stakes tests and compare their results with the lab's logged raw scores. If the two match across three consecutive yearly cycles, with no rerun in any of the three samples differing from the logged score beyond a tolerance fixed in advance, the outside cross-check of incidents against deployment scale and complaints should be dropped. The presumption for missing records stays either way. It costs a lab that keeps its records nothing.\n\nReaders can watch for three things as this law is written. Does the bill name who drafts the field standards? Does the inspector, rather than the lab, sign off on cleaned summaries? Does it fund independent reruns? The open question is concrete. Which observation would move the Accelerationist? And when a lab cites its own summary as proof it met the standard of the day, with the original gone, who answers for that summary?",
